WHY THIS MATTERS EVEN BEFORE THE RUN
A skeptic might ask why an unverified formal specification is worth publishing at all. Two reasons:
First, the specification itself is a real technical artifact — writing the FSM formally, precisely, and (in this version) in syntactically valid TLA+ is work that a stochastic transformer architecture cannot meaningfully do, because there is no small, closed state machine to specify in the first place. That structural difference — having something checkable to write down — is the actual claim, independent of whether the checking has been run yet.
Second, publishing the target verification program before running it, and being explicit about what's proven versus what's expected, is itself a form of accountability: readers can watch this program get completed (or not) rather than take a finished claim on faith.
We think of trust in AI systems as sitting on a spectrum rather than in three fixed tiers:
Assertion — "we believe it is safe," with no supporting evidence offered.
Empirical evidence — "we tested it extensively," which is where most current safety-certified AI systems sit today.
Formal verification — a mechanically checked proof that a specification (not necessarily the full physical implementation) satisfies stated properties.
ST-T1024's architecture is designed to make the third tier reachable, in a way that stochastic, high-parameter-count architectures structurally are not — there is no small enough state space to check exhaustively. Reaching that tier for this specific system still requires completing the program in Sections 4–5. This paper documents the design intent and the specification; it does not yet claim the completed proof.
